 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of frames $N$, as each frame is processed once. However, the cost per frame depends on the regression and PnP steps. Freezing old parameters reduces the optimization burden per step [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Scales linearly with $N$ as the training buffer grows. The scene regressor memory footprint is constant [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Robust initialization for poses in video sequences; avoids local minima associated with global optimization; efficient as it freezes stable parameters [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires temporal order or a known sequence; susceptible to drift if early frames are misregistered; not suitable for unordered image collections without a pre-defined graph [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Video-based reconstruction, datasets with smooth camera motion (e.g., CO3D, HO3D, LLFF) [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Unordered image sets, scenes with large baseline jumps (>20 degrees rotation) between adjacent frames without intermediate steps [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of frames $N$, but each registration step involves expensive 3D point estimation and RANSAC. Total training time is significantly higher than standard NeRF due to iterative pose optimization [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Scales with the number of registered images and the scene regressor size. No significant additional memory overhead compared to standard NeRF, except for storing intermediate 3D points during registration [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Robust to large initial pose errors by leveraging temporal smoothness; avoids local minima associated with joint optimization of all poses and radiance fields simultaneously [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires temporal order of images; performance degrades if camera motion is not smooth or if there are large gaps between frames [S2]. Accumulated errors in poses may require final refinement [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Video sequences or ordered image collections where camera motion is smooth [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Unordered image collections or scenes with rapid, non-smooth camera motion [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Incremental Registration was introduced in the context of joint pose and radiance field optimization to address the non-convexity of simultaneous optimization [S2]. It builds on the observation that camera motions in videos are smooth, allowing adjacent poses to be initialized from one another [S2]. The method is closely related to BARF (Blending Arbitrary Reference Frames) but differs in its incremental, frame-by-frame addition strategy rather than frequency-based blending [S2]. It is used in datasets like CO3D, HO3D, LLFF, and Mip-NeRF360 [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Incremental Registration was proposed in the ICON (Incremental CONfidence) paper by Wang et al. (2024) [S2]. It addresses the breaking point of 20-degree rotation difference observed in prior work (Wang et al., 2021b) by relying on the intuition that camera motions in videos are smooth [S2]. The method builds on BARF (Blending Arbitrary Reference Frames) settings, using coarse-to-fine positional encoding strategies [S2]. It is used in datasets like CO3D, HO3D, LLFF, and Mip-NeRF360 [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
